63 #edb/ref 17592186129324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130322 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf A$ by its standard matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/ac/ac86756a955ff34f5cd438373385537d40902ed764c24dcf70aebe0170509233.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{A}$ by computing the matrix products as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=i+2\\,j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b562af85ac2ad0f4871105d61b43aa8e27d215a4006c0ee3371cd673b2dd9579.png)\n\nThe transformation rotates the square by an angle of \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}1}\\right) \\approx 63.4^\\circ,\n$$\n\nthen scales it by a scale factor of \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{{\\color{blue}1}^2 + {\\color{red}2}^2} = \\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nIn fact, any matrix of the form \n![](images/de/de48c49adcb549b17f02f1f40d04d7817b6e5bd255ee390317be8da0a0b9bb3c.svg)\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ and $\\color{red}b$ are real numbers (not both equal to zero simultaneously) is a matrix that rotates and then scales. \nThese types of matrices are called **rotation-scaling matrices**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130322 #edb/ref 1065 2012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130322 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81772221-4f86-554a-a3a0-b51dc4c6d12e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130322 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130323 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen that we can write a rotation-scaling matrix $A$ is the product of a rotation and a scaling matrix:\n\n![](images/2f/2f8ddb3f34a2e05e7d0d222dbe693328d7a9d8e7563c9fb44933231fdc453aa5.svg)\n\nSo, the natural question now is, how does a rotation-scaling matrix \n![](images/77/77cb6dbe3ef4dd36908e6f4a93ac5133c25d81af4502f3a396e2b0e4f9716fc8.svg)\nrelate to the individual rotation and scaling matrices?\n\nFirst, setting the two equal to each other, we get\n\n![](images/cd/cd6202c0ab3e908d20fa1ceca61f3a7d1baceaa7187a64e7c2a7030b0fa8f0e2.svg)\n\nFrom this, we can find the scale factor and angle of rotation of a rotation-scaling matrix. First, notice that we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo find $r$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we can square and then add the two equations above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the scale factor $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} = \\sqrt{\\det{A}}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the rotation angle $\\theta$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we start with the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{b}{a}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac ba \\right).\n$$\n\nIt's also possible to recover both the original scaling *and* rotation matrix once the scale factor $r$ is known. Let's see how that works." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130323 #edb/ref 1065 2014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130323 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fb2c1d9-0856-55ca-945a-8e01f2e58902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130323 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Scale Factor and Rotation Angle Of a Rotation-Scaling Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we construct a particular rotation-scaling matrix $A?$\n\nFirst, recall that rotation matrices are of the form\n![](images/50/5027e188171ed6898ca18d22afc5fa4cadd1c9b95fa6d5f3ca738c1365884495.svg)\nHere, the matrix $R$ rotates a vector by an angle of $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nScaling matrices are of the form\n![](images/b7/b73fad8b6f2fbd691d60b2b326ac5a1affd32dac1f2647a7096a44222defc817.svg)\nThe scaling matrix $S$ scales a vector by a scale factor of $r,$ where the origin is the center of enlargement. \n\nWe can write any rotation-scaling matrix $A$ as a product of a scaling matrix and a rotation matrix: \n\n$$\nA=SR\n$$\n\nOr, more explicitly:\n![](images/66/66f21dce4b71254563eea309f73587cc4e6d4a8b788a23e85425b31145ed191c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1065 4390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6a451bc3-5ec6-543a-911f-04a39b6fdcf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1067 "Constructing a Rotation-Scaling Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a42ab3804fa207390da60307a2af967d075c0ae7d1dd108f6db809540ffa6e6a.png)\n\nThe vectors $v$ and $Av$ are shown above. If $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then $A=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "031a2cb1-243c-5ae8-a8d9-6bc7aa6f524e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369769 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the scale factor and $\\theta $ is the angle of rotation. \n\nAccording to the diagram, $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369770 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95113e42-02b3-5065-b438-928ab09d1c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369771 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369771 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369771 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "730e2b8b-1451-51ad-bd2a-40ca7c9a62c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369772 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369772 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369772 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b10f497-fa87-5f2c-87c1-ba8d257a9554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369773 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cfe920ee-91cf-508a-847b-bb3ec48f62c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369774 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369774 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369774 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "511c1518-2152-5c09-aece-ccfe590a316f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369775 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369775 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369775 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "443a5ca8-70d6-54a0-98e0-0e7b64be25a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8febf6527cbacf1e328dd7d50180deeab66fc29d4b4391de3e85b7db22b4568c.png)\n\nThe vectors $v$ and $Av$ are shown above. If $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then $A=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5b160e6-d77c-511f-89aa-10fd8ad6198f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394207 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the scale factor and $\\theta $ the angle of rotation. \n\nAccording to the diagram, $r=3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394208 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "be0b9c50-e64e-58c9-9a7f-a1416b9581d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394209 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394209 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394209 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "51328703-fbe2-5d04-852e-d6672e539570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394210 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394210 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394210 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1c1c7d5-d4f2-523a-92d8-2bf34edf6009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394211 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394211 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2eacdc8-9970-5269-aa34-69d6b10aca40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394212 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394212 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394212 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aba58c5f-7269-5f4e-9e46-bf3c0ffef660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394213 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394213 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394213 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3cb67527-0d4f-5911-a92c-e407ba5f8bab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f9ad045dc1da5464394ae75061ff7b613334c39d6f5a27d5b23eccef13c339c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bebbf60-a47c-5b85-9c8e-87fefe6e5ddd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394256 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd2fda4038ae2342b37df91a520a58463ce70748c143c6619968da71411aff1c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394257 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e51414d9-147d-5c81-b876-34c3bdd3b99b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394258 #edb/ref 1080 "images/9f/9f2b2163f62915ab2a88d96b2e9c40fa1ef5db3f869df495dfd92ac839c5cb2c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394258 